  "conclusion": "The argument is invalid. The first step that does not follow is step (4).",
  "confidence": 0.86,
  "reasoning": "Step (4) purports to infer from (2) and (3) that if a patient ranks first on some reasonable criterion then any fair rule must place that patient first. But (2) says: “A rule ignores a criterion exactly when the rule’s outcome would be the same no matter how patients scored on that criterion.” Taking the negation, “the rule does not ignore the criterion” means only that there exist some hypothetical changes in that criterion’s scores that would change the rule’s outcome. It does not mean that, for the actual score profile, the person who is top on that criterion must be selected. In short, not-ignoring a criterion (sensitivity to it in some cases) is weaker than giving absolute priority to the top-ranked patient on that criterion in every actual instance. Step (4) illegitimately replaces “rule takes the criterion into account (is not invariant to it)” with “rule must always put the criterion’s top-ranked patient first.” That shift is not a valid deductive inference.",
  "uncertainty": "This assessment treats the premises and the word “ignore” exactly as stated and uses standard quantifier logic. If the committee intended a stronger reading of (2) or (3) (for example, that ‘‘taking a criterion into account’’ means treating top-ranked agents on that criterion as decisive priority), the analysis would change. The informal phrase “reasonable criterion of medical desert” is normative and vague; my logical point is independent of the moral attractiveness of the premises."
}
